<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Pitt the Younger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1759">1759</a>–<a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Pitt the Younger was Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> from 1783 to 1801 and again from 1804 to 1806, taking office at the age of 24. Son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-elder">William Pitt the Elder</a>, he restored the public finances after the American war, introduced income tax and led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> against revolutionary and Napoleonic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He brought about the union with Ireland in 1801 but resigned when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> blocked Catholic emancipation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger.jpg" alt="William Pitt the Younger" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Hoppner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OlderPittThe_Younger.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Whigs dominated government until the 1760s, when George III sought a more active role; long ministries followed under Lord North from 1770 and <b>William Pitt the Younger</b> from 1783.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The prime minister, <b>William Pitt the Younger</b>, and the chief secretary, Lord Castlereagh, concluded that only union with Great Britain could secure Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The rebellion of the Society of United Irishmen in 1798, in which many thousands died and a French force landed in County Mayo, convinced <b>William Pitt the Younger</b> that union was needed to secure Ireland during the war with revolutionary France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Hanoverian kings Sir Robert Walpole, in office from 1721 to 1742, became in effect the first prime minister, yet monarchs still shaped policy: George III’s opposition to Catholic emancipation forced <b>William Pitt the Younger</b> to resign in 1801.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Until the 1830s, however, the king still chose ministers and could sustain them, as George III sustained <b>William Pitt the Younger</b> against a hostile Commons majority in 1783–1784.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>William Pitt the Younger</b>'s income tax, set at two shillings in the pound on higher incomes, received royal assent to fund the war against Revolutionary France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-9">9 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kent, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.19,0.73&amp;year=1789" aria-label="Kent on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4567 390 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William Pitt the Younger"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5017 660h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5017 660h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.19,0.73&amp;year=1789">What was here in 1789? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
